1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
 Now we're going to represent these relationships in the database.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:05,720
 And again, what we're trying to solve here

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,080
 is this notion of database normalization.

4
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,320
 Third normal form.

5
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,480
 There is so much theory, right?

6
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:15,100
 But in this lecture, I'm just going to condense this down

7
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:19,300
 to don't replicate string data and use what are called keys.

8
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:22,360
 Use integer keys to point at those things.

9
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,720
 And we're going to use these integers then to point.

10
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:27,600
 So assign each row an integer and then we're going to point

11
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,600
 from one row to another using those integers.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,880
 And so we're going to add these special key columns

13
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,200
 to each of the tables and help in the database

14
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,960
 will even give us help managing those.

15
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:41,240
 Now, and so we still need to keep track of, you know,

16
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,480
 who is the creator of the album,

17
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:44,920
 which album a track belongs to.

18
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,440
 We've got to create these relationships

19
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,680
 and we have to come up with ways to store those relationships.

20
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,440
 And so the idea is, is we're going to have a column

21
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,280
 and a table which is the key column.

22
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:58,200
 And we're going to call this the ID column.

23
00:00:58,200 --> 00:00:59,080
 And so this is a row.

24
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,420
 It might have many bits of data here,

25
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:02,920
 but in this case, it's just the name of an artist.

26
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:06,040
 So this album is going to belong to an artist.

27
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,680
 And we're going to assign a number inside the database.

28
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:12,760
 And so that Led Zeppelin is one and ACDC is two.

29
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:15,760
 And so we have this key, this is called a primary key.

30
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,980
 And then later when we want to say that the Who Made Who album

31
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:24,540
 really was done by ACDC, we put the number two in.

32
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:28,240
 And so the difference here is instead of saying ACDC,

33
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:31,040
 in this record, we just put the number two

34
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,760
 once we've established this number.

35
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:35,720
 So we assign keys and then we have these pointers

36
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:39,440
 that point back and so that's how we model a relationship

37
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:42,120
 with these small integer numbers.

38
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,680
 And so there are three basic kind of keys that we use.

39
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,320
 One is the primary key and that is that little ID column

40
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:49,620
 that is just a number.

41
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:51,820
 But once we give Led Zeppelin the number one,

42
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,120
 Led Zeppelin has got the key one

43
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:57,180
 for the rest of that database.

44
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,240
 The logical key is the text area that we use

45
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,280
 that you might look up.

46
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:03,780
 So the title of the band or the title of the album,

47
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:05,160
 that's logical key.

48
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:06,800
 And then the foreign key is one of these keys

49
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,940
 that is really pointing to the primary key of another row.

50
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,100
 So that's called a foreign key.

51
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,280
 And you might think that you want to use something

52
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:18,280
 like an email address as the primary key

53
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,080
 for a user table or something like that.

54
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,360
 The logical key should always be separate

55
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,180
 and there should always be a primary key,

56
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:25,420
 that integer number.

57
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:27,100
 'Cause things like logical keys do change,

58
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:28,740
 people do get new email addresses.

59
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,480
 And if you've got that email address

60
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,320
 as a foreign key pointing all over the place,

61
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:34,560
 it doesn't work out so well.

62
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:37,200
 And so that's why you use these small integer numbers

63
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,000
 that have no meaning outside.

64
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,320
 So sometimes if you're on a system and you see a URL

65
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,800
 and you see some number like 422,016,

66
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,240
 you're like, oh, that turns out to probably be

67
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,240
 my primary key in their database.

68
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:50,440
 So sometimes you can look in a URL

69
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:52,720
 and you can see these primary keys in the URL,

70
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,640
 but they don't mean anything outside

71
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:55,960
 of that particular system.

72
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,920
 So like I said, a foreign key is a key

73
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,000
 that is really pointing at a row in a different table.

74
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,860
 And so we have, the album has a primary key for it,

75
00:03:05,860 --> 00:03:09,760
 but the artist_id points to a row in the artist table,

76
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:11,100
 as we will soon see.

77
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:13,760
 I have a naming convention.

78
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,440
 And in my naming convention, on this lecture,

79
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,440
 I use ID for the primary key.

80
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:22,460
 And then artist_id, I use uppercase for the table names.

81
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:26,240
 And then artist_id says, this is a key,

82
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:29,200
 this is just a key that points to the ID key

83
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:30,600
 of the artist table.

84
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:31,480
 And so that's what I do.

85
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:33,600
 So you'll see, in all my stuff I'll use that.

86
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,360
 It's a convention.

87
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:38,020
 It's not something SQL forces you to do,

88
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:40,000
 but you will find when you go to organizations

89
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,120
 and work on their databases,

90
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,080
 these conventions are very important,

91
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:45,120
 so I can do something and you can understand

92
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:47,480
 the rules in which I created it.

93
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,880
 Some of these, you'll find this used by some people,

94
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:51,760
 you'll find completely different conventions

95
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:52,580
 and that'll be okay.

96
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:55,200
 Whatever convention your organization uses,

97
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,200
 learn that convention.

98
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,600
 So now we're going to talk about how we put these keys in

99
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,440
 and then how we actually make the connections

100
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:05,280
 from one row to another row.

